---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DispatchSignup from '../components/DispatchSignup.astro';
import { SITE, PILLARS } from '../data/site';
import { IMAGES } from '../data/images';
import { CITIES, COUNTRY_COUNT } from '../data/cities';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About Chandler | ${SITE.name}`}
  description="A Canadian from Atlantic Canada. In May 2022 I booked a one-month Airbnb in Tallinn, Estonia — and discovered that solo travel wasn't just something I wanted to do. It was something I needed to do."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-1">About</p>
      <h1 class="display-xl mb-2">Chandler</h1>
      <p class="body-lg measure">
        A Canadian from Atlantic Canada. In May 2022 I booked a one-month Airbnb in Tallinn, Estonia
        — and discovered that solo travel wasn't just something I wanted to do. It was something I
        needed to do.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ─── The story ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="split split--wide-gap">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-2">The Story</p>
          <div class="stack">
            <p class="body-lg">
              It started in May 2022. I booked a month-long Airbnb on Narva Mantee in Tallinn,
              Estonia — a long, straight road running east from the old town. I had always known I
              wanted to travel, but that trip was when I realised it wasn't just a want. It was a
              need.
            </p>
            <p class="body-lg">
              I walked everywhere. I found the medieval old town, which is one of the best-preserved
              in Europe and somehow still feels lived-in. I took day trips to Tartu and Pärnu. I took
              the ferry to Helsinki and stood in the city feeling, for the first time, exactly what
              I was chasing with the style side of this project. I went out, met strangers, ended up
              at an apartment party at 7am, and walked home at 8am as the city was waking up. I met
              people everywhere I went.
            </p>
            <p class="body-lg">
              That trip gave me something I hadn't fully had before: confidence. The knowledge that
              I could go somewhere completely alone and not just survive it but genuinely thrive. I
              fell in love with the European approach to life — laidback but functional, rooted in
              history, intentional in a way that's hard to find elsewhere.
            </p>
            <p class="body-lg">
              What followed was Iceland in September 2022, a four-month run across twenty-one cities
              in 2023, Iberia in 2024, and a return to Scandinavia in 2025 — {CITIES.length} cities
              across {COUNTRY_COUNT} countries so far. CityboundNomad is the honest documentation of
              all of it. The cities, the style, the freedom — they were always connected. Tallinn
              just made it clear.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:1rem">
          <img
            src={IMAGES.journey}
            alt="Chandler in a European cafe"
            style="width:100%; height:380px; object-fit:cover; object-position:center 30%"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
          <img
            src={IMAGES.travel}
            alt="A European street scene"
            style="width:100%; height:220px; object-fit:cover"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <!-- ─── The framework ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-1">The Framework</p>
      <h2 class="display-lg mb-2" style="max-width:500px">Why three pillars?</h2>
      <p class="body-lg measure-wide mb-4">
        Most travel blogs are just travel blogs. Most style blogs are just style blogs.
        CityboundNomad is built on the observation that these things are connected — that the way
        you travel informs how you dress, and the way you dress reflects how you think about
        possessions and permanence.
      </p>

      <div class="cell-grid cell-grid--tight">
        {
          PILLARS.map((pillar) => (
            <a class="pillar-card card-hover" href={pillar.href}>
              <p class="pillar-card__num" style="font-size:3rem">
                {pillar.num}
              </p>
              <p class="pillar-label mb-1">{pillar.label}</p>
              <p class="body-sm">{pillar.short}</p>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <!-- ─── Transparency ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="measure-wide">
        <p class="eyebrow mb-1">Transparency</p>
        <h2 class="display-lg mb-2">How this works</h2>
        <div class="stack">
          <p class="body-lg">
            CityboundNomad is built to earn through affiliate links (accommodation, tours, and
            clothing or gear brands), occasional brand partnerships, and eventually digital
            products. Every affiliate link is disclosed and marked. Nothing gets linked that I
            haven't actually used — see the <a href="/affiliate-disclosure" style="color:var(--c-accent)">affiliate disclosure</a>.
          </p>
          <p class="body-lg">
            The rule for partnerships is simple: nothing that requires me to say something I don't
            believe. The Dispatch does not carry sponsored messages. That's not a policy imposed on
            me — it's a preference, and it's the reason the newsletter is worth subscribing to.
          </p>
          <p class="body-lg">
            Sections of this site are deliberately empty. Where there is no stay review, no gear
            review, or no essay, it's because the writing hasn't been done yet — not because there's
            nothing to fill the space with. Filling it with something approximate would defeat the
            point of the whole project.
          </p>
          <p class="body-lg">
            CityboundNomad is an entity of Strandway Systems, based in Nova Scotia, Canada.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="callout mt-3">
          “Fewer readers who trust me beats more readers who don't.”
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="container" style="padding-bottom:5rem">
    <DispatchSignup
      headline="Follow the journey"
      subtext="The Dispatch is the best way to follow along. One email per week, every Friday."
      source="about"
    />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
